## Captured company-number quality

The same capture has 2,467 raw rows with a non-null company number, all with source kind `Company`. Of these, 2,197 match eight digits or two letters followed by six digits after trimming. This pattern was an exploratory count, not a complete Companies House validator. Other values include `527227`, `0967733649`, `123`, and `NI 016363`.

Two primary documentation sources were read. No company-record API was called.

- [Companies House number-entry guidance](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company-lookup/search?forward=/auth-code-requests/company/%7BcompanyNumber%7D/confirm) specifies an eight-character number and instructs users to add leading zeros to shorter values. It gives a numeric example. A proposed numeric padding rule can cite this guidance; it is not proof that the source supplied the correct number.
- [Companies House URI guide](https://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/426891/uniformResourceIdentifiersCustomerGuide.pdf), version 1.1, says prefixes use uppercase and lists legacy forms including `R0`. Its old prefix list is not a current exhaustive validator.

Design consequence: distinguish mechanical canonicalization from registry verification. Do not implement a guessed exhaustive prefix validator. Leave unusual or ambiguous numbers unresolved in the first version and retain their raw spelling. Deciding whether to pad short numeric values is an explicit policy choice supported by the entry guidance, not an API lookup.
